## Greeting\
<p>\
v01: Paul, called to be an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God, and Sosthenes our brother,\
v02: To the church of God that is in Corinth, to those who have been made holy in Christ Jesus, called to be a holy people, together with all who in every place call upon the name of our Lord Jesus Christ\'97both their Lord and ours:\
v03: Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.\
</p>\
\
## Thanksgiving\
<p>\
v04: I give thanks to my God always for you, because of the grace of God that has been given to you in Christ Jesus.\
v05: For in every way you have been enriched in him\'97in speech and knowledge of every kind\'97\
v06: just as the testimony of Christ has been confirmed among you,\
v07: so that you are not lacking in any spiritual gift as you wait for the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ.\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v08: He will also strengthen you to the end, so that you may be blameless on the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.\
v09: God is faithful; by him you were called into the fellowship of his Son, Jesus Christ our Lord.\
</p>\
\
## Divisions in the Church\
<p>\
v10: Now I urge you, brothers and sisters, by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that all of you be in agreement, and that there be no divisions among you, but that you be united in the same mind and the same purpose.\
v11: For it has been reported to me by Chloe\'92s people that there are quarrels among you, my brothers and sisters.\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v12: What I mean is this: each of you says, \'93I belong to Paul,\'94 or \'93I belong to Apollos,\'94 or \'93I belong to Cephas,\'94 or \'93I belong to Christ.\'94\
v13: Has Christ been divided? Was Paul crucified for you? Or were you baptized in the name of Paul?\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v14: I thank God that I baptized none of you except Crispus and Gaius,\
v15: so that no one can say that you were baptized in my name.\
v16: (I did baptize also the household of Stephanas; beyond that, I do not know whether I baptized anyone else.)\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v17: For Christ did not send me to baptize but to proclaim the good news\'97and not with wisdom of speech, lest the cross of Christ be emptied of its power.\
</p>\
\
## Christ the Power and Wisdom of God\
<p>\
v18: For the word of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is the power of God.\
v19: For it is written,\
\'93I will destroy the wisdom of the wise,\
and the discernment of the discerning I will thwart.\'94\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v20: Where is the wise one? Where is the scribe? Where is the debater of this age? Has not God made foolish the wisdom of the world?\
v21: For since, in the wisdom of God, the world did not know God through wisdom, God decided, through the foolishness of what is proclaimed, to save those who believe.\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v22: For Jews demand signs and Greeks seek wisdom,\
v23: but we proclaim Christ crucified\'97a stumbling block to Jews and foolishness to Gentiles,\
v24: but to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God.\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v25: For God\'92s foolishness is wiser than human wisdom, and God\'92s weakness is stronger than human strength.\
</p>\
\
## God\'92s Choice of the Lowly\
<p>\
v26: Consider your own calling, brothers and sisters: not many of you were wise by human standards, not many were powerful, not many were of noble birth.\
v27: But God chose what is foolish in the world to shame the wise; God chose what is weak in the world to shame the strong;\
v28: God chose what is low and despised in the world\'97things that are not\'97to bring to nothing things that are,\
v29: so that no one might boast in the presence of God.\
</p>\
\
<p>\
v30: He is the source of your life in Christ Jesus, who became for us wisdom from God, and righteousness and sanctification and redemption,\
v31: in order that, as it is written, \'93Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord.\'94\
</p>\
\
## Notes\
v1: Paul grounds his authority not in personal achievement but in divine calling. The pairing with Sosthenes emphasizes shared witness rather than solitary leadership.\
v2: The church is addressed both as already \'93made holy\'94 and as \'93called\'94 into holiness. Paul holds status and vocation together without collapsing one into the other.\
v3: Grace precedes peace. The order reflects Paul\'92s conviction that reconciliation and wholeness flow from divine gift, not human effort.\
v4\'967: Thanksgiving focuses not on moral maturity but on God\'92s generosity. Paul names real gifts before addressing real problems, framing correction within gratitude.\
v8\'969: Assurance rests on God\'92s faithfulness rather than the community\'92s consistency. The future is anchored in God\'92s action, not human reliability.\
v10: Unity is described in terms of shared orientation (\'93mind\'94 and \'93purpose\'94), not uniformity of opinion or personality.\
v11\'9612: Divisions are exposed through reported speech. Paul allows the factions\'92 slogans to indict themselves.\
v13: The rhetorical questions destabilize loyalty structures. Allegiance to leaders is exposed as a distortion of baptismal identity.\
v17: Paul contrasts proclamation with rhetorical display. The danger is not wisdom itself, but wisdom that displaces the cross.\
v18: The present tense (\'93are being saved,\'94 \'93are perishing\'94) frames salvation as an unfolding reality rather than a completed transaction.\
v19\'9620: Scripture is invoked to relativize every age\'92s confidence in its own insight. Wisdom is measured by God\'92s purposes, not cultural approval.\
v21: The paradox is intentional: God\'92s wisdom does not bypass foolishness but works through it.\
v22\'9624: Competing expectations\'97signs and wisdom\'97are both unsettled by a crucified Messiah.\
v25: Paul does not redefine foolishness and weakness as virtues in themselves; he redefines where true wisdom and strength are located.\
v26\'9629: The community\'92s social makeup becomes theological evidence. God\'92s choosing undermines boasting by rearranging the grounds of honor.\
v30: Salvation is described relationally (\'93in Christ Jesus\'94) and expansively, gathering multiple dimensions without hierarchy.\
v31: The chapter closes by redirecting pride. Boasting is not eliminated, but reoriented toward God alone.\
\
## Vocabulary\
v1: \uc0\u954 \u955 \u951 \u964 \u972 \u962  (kl\u275 tos)\
\'93Called.\'94 The term denotes divine initiative rather than human qualification. Paul\'92s identity begins in response, not achievement.\
v2: \uc0\u7937 \u947 \u953 \u940 \u950 \u969  (hagiaz\u333 )\
\'93To make holy.\'94 The perfect participle indicates a completed act with ongoing effect\'97holiness as given status, not earned condition.\
v9: \uc0\u954 \u959 \u953 \u957 \u969 \u957 \u943 \u945  (koin\u333 nia)\
\'93Fellowship\'94 or \'93participation.\'94 The word emphasizes shared life and mutual belonging, not merely association.\
v10: \uc0\u963 \u967 \u943 \u963 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u945  (schismata)\
\'93Divisions\'94 or \'93tears.\'94 The imagery suggests something once whole being pulled apart.\
v17: \uc0\u963 \u959 \u966 \u943 \u945  \u955 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u965  (sophia logou)\
\'93Wisdom of speech.\'94 The phrase points to rhetorical sophistication rather than wisdom itself.\
v18: \uc0\u955 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u963 \u964 \u945 \u965 \u961 \u959 \u8166  (logos tou staurou)\
\'93The word of the cross.\'94 Not merely a message about the cross, but speech shaped by its meaning.\
v21: \uc0\u956 \u969 \u961 \u943 \u945  (m\u333 ria)\
\'93Foolishness.\'94 The term names perceived absurdity, not actual lack of sense.\
v24: \uc0\u948 \u973 \u957 \u945 \u956 \u953 \u962  (dynamis)\
\'93Power.\'94 Often associated with divine action, especially where human capacity fails.\
v26: \uc0\u963 \u959 \u966 \u959 \u943  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u8048  \u963 \u940 \u961 \u954 \u945  (sophoi kata sarka)\
\'93Wise according to the flesh.\'94 Wisdom measured by social and cultural standards rather than divine perspective.\
v30: \uc0\u7952 \u958  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  (ex autou)\
\'93From him.\'94 The phrase grounds the community\'92s life in God as source, not self-generation.\
v31: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u965 \u967 \u940 \u959 \u956 \u945 \u953  (kauchaomai)\
\'93To boast.\'94 Paul reframes boasting as testimony\'97speech that locates confidence outside the self.\
